Most workloads are launch-bound, not bandwidth-bound.** 11 of the 16 sizes finish in under 10 µs, where CPU dispatch dominates. I measured the components rather than guessing: Triton's `JITFunction.run` costs ~11 µs of Python, `torch.cuda.current_stream().cuda_stream` ~2.4 µs, and `@torch.no_grad()` ~1.05 µs. Replacing these with a pre-compiled direct launcher, the raw stream accessor, and no decorator took the floor from **9.7 µs → 6.0 µs**.\n\n## Where the ceiling is\n\nI checked whether the remaining gap to SOL was real headroom by measuring the machine's own limits: a pure 8-byte copy reaches 6739 GB/s and a no-op kernel launch costs 3.7 µs. My kernel runs at **~7100–7400 GB/s (89–93% of the 8 TB/s bound)** — *faster than a plain copy* — and its 4.35 µs launch is essentially the HIP floor that torch's own operators also pay. Both limits are the hardware's, not the kernel's. Cache modifiers and block/warp retuning moved things by under 1%, within the noise of this shared GPU (I found 107 GB of HBM held by other processes, which is why I timed with interleaved A/B and CUDA-graph capture rather than trusting wall-clock).\n\n## One real bug caught late\n\nRobustness testing found that **strided inputs silently produced wrong answers** — the kernels index memory linearly. The workloads are all contiguous, so this never fires, but \"silently wrong\" is the worst failure mode available. A 0.16 µs contiguity guard now degrades that case to merely slower.\n\nFinal state: bit-exact across 384 configurations (all 16 sizes × normal/large/tiny/wide/denormal/inf-nan inputs × 4 seeds), plus non-default streams, offset views, and `requires_grad` inputs. Verified loading from a clean tree, since that is how the scoring run will read it. Scratch files removed.","ttft_ms":3501,"ttft_stream_ms":3500,"time_to_request_ms":84,"type":"result","duration_ms":1797395,"uuid":"79de99e5-62b5-4e23-9494-29eb4992f578"}
